+# Movement 008 cold-read review
+
+## Read status and coverage
+
+This is a **fresh-context simulated cold read**, not a real audience measurement. I read only the seven supplied manuscript files, chapters 47–53, in their declared order. I had no editorial canon evidence, so canon and reserved-disclosure findings below are necessarily limited to apparent manuscript consistency.
+
+The supplied movement is **textually incomplete**. Nine `[[TIER-B: ... coordinator to supply protected text]]` blocks remain in chapters 49–53, including the closing exchange of chapter 53. The narrative arc is readable around most of them, but those blocks withhold dialogue and expose production instructions at several decisive beats. Scores therefore describe the manuscript as supplied, not the likely quality of the completed scenes.
+
+## Unscored reader response
+
+My interest first caught in chapter 47 when Karis finds that her marker stone has been removed and the turf restored to make the ground agree with an official sheet. That turns a quiet return journey into a forensic problem. It caught much harder in chapter 48 at “Shale-backs. On a hill. Over the only water in three days.” The problem is concrete, the rules are quickly understandable, and the solution requires Cael, Lira, Brom, and Teague to contribute different kinds of judgment. The confrontation at the shelf in chapter 50 then converts the movement’s recurring ideas—counting, restraint, ground-reading, and the difference between a person and an office—into action.
+
+Interest slipped in two places. The first is the opening third of chapter 47, where many remembered sites, day counts, unnamed roles, and prior incidents arrive before a cold reader can reliably sort the twelve travelers. The second is the long evidence assembly in chapter 52. Its discoveries are strong, but “the hide,” “the flat,” several classes of sheets, tapes, columns, seals, the brass, and the folded paper compete for working memory. The unresolved bracketed instructions cause a more basic break: they repeatedly turn story back into an editing session.
+
+Cael matters most to me. His emerging competence is not merely an increase in force; it is an increase in judgment. He learns to read ground, holds Teague’s left for the promised three breaths, receives a dangerous new capability, refuses to fire it, recognizes where its cost would land, and keeps other people’s secrets even when disclosure might ease his own burden. Karis is nearly as important because she supplies the movement’s intellectual and ethical standard: evidence may point toward a wanted conclusion, but she will not create the missing line herself.
+
+I expect the next event to be the approach through the square gap and entry into—or confrontation at—the oldest site on the line. I also expect Cael’s Force-adjacent capability and vulnerable ribs to become an immediate tactical problem, not merely a later progression note. I would continue. The world’s material specificity, the group’s quiet attachments, and the promise of the oldest site all pull strongly, but I would want the missing protected dialogue restored before treating this as a finished reading experience.
+
+## Fluent thirteen-year-old lens
+
+These are editorial judgments through a lens, not demographic research.
+
+| Dimension | Score | Exact passage location | Reason | Confidence |
+|---|---:|---|---|---|
+| Opening pull | 7/10 | Chapter 47, opening through “being asked to look was heavier work than looking” | The silent file, Cael’s new duty, and the old man keeping the road create curiosity. The immediate pressure is subtle, however, and the many implied earlier events make the entry slower than the later chapters. | Medium |
+| Keep reading | 8/10 | Chapter 53, Seln’s report beginning “It is nothing that I saw,” then the final lamp scene | The empty gap, the oldest site, and Cael’s reason for wanting its middle create strong forward pressure. The final bracketed block withholds the actual closing exchange and weakens the delivered payoff. | High |
+| Interest / freshness | 9/10 | Chapter 48, the shale-back water scene from “Never be below them. Never be loud below them” through the enlarged seep | A herd over the only water, solved through behavior, geology, restraint, and teamwork, feels specific and memorable rather than like a generic fight. | High |
+| Clarity / flow | 5/10 | Chapter 47, the opening file inventory; chapter 50, from Brom taking the bolts through the crossing of stake thirty-four | Individual actions are usually clear, but “the boy,” “the old man,” “the broad man,” “the tall man,” and named characters are hard to map under pressure. Unexplained terms and the bracketed production notes make one-pass comprehension inconsistent. | High |
+| Character attachment | 8/10 | Chapter 51, Teague watching the sleeping boy and recognizing “whose boots a boy watched” | Care is expressed through choices rather than speeches: the boy holds a fallen man’s head, Teague reconsiders how he uses trust, and Cael keeps the place behind Teague’s shoulder full. | High |
+| Humor / warmth | 8/10 | Chapter 48, “It took me for a stone.” / “It’s not the first”; chapter 53, the two sticks sharing one crack | The humor is brief and character-based, while practical gestures carry warmth without making the danger sentimental. Some dry jokes may pass quickly for a younger reader amid the dense context. | High |
+| Action / suspense | 8/10 | Chapter 50, from “Brom walked” through the watch retreating via the gap | Positions, the crossbow count, Teague’s three breaths, the iron line, and the retreat route make the contest tactical and consequential. Role ambiguity and missing dialogue briefly disrupt an otherwise excellent sequence. | High |
+| Progression payoff | 7/10 | Chapter 50, Cael receiving the “Force-adjacent” thirteenth; chapter 53, his unfired test at the slab | Cael gains a capability, understands its mechanism, identifies its bodily cost, and chooses restraint. The payoff is meaningful, though the taxonomy of Path, sigil, read, Pressure, and “not a Path” is difficult to reconstruct from this movement alone. | Medium |
+| Connection | 8/10 | The daily logs ending chapters 47–53, especially the carried counts for Vastin, Oryn, deployments, and the thirteenth | Injuries, promises, water, equipment, dates, and discoveries visibly travel forward. The repeated numerical bookkeeping rewards attention, although the shifting references to eleven and twelve travelers can feel uncertain. | Medium |
+| Read-aloud quality | 8/10 | Chapter 50, “That was one breath. / A stride in front of him, a man was being put straight”; chapter 53, the two gateposts image | Repetition, short landings, and concrete comparisons give the prose strong cadence. Some long sentences carry several pronouns or embedded references, and the literal editorial brackets are unperformable. | High |
+
+## Adult genre-reader lens
+
+| Dimension | Score | Exact passage location | Reason | Confidence |
+|---|---:|---|---|---|
+| Opening pull | 8/10 | Chapter 47, from the silent ridge march to the replaced marker stone | The opening trusts prior momentum while quickly establishing Cael’s new observational role. The altered ground and falsified official measure supply a strong investigative hook. | High |
+| Keep reading | 8/10 | Chapter 52, the two contradictory sealed slips; chapter 53, Karis’s lamp and thumbnail ring | The institutional “crack,” the oldest-last line, and the unoccupied gap create layered forward pressure. The absent final dialogue prevents the close from landing at full strength. | High |
+| Interest / freshness | 9/10 | Chapter 52, the one-blanket reconstruction and “They fail the way a hundred lamps fail when one wire’s cut” | Bureaucratic forms, field measurement, physical ruins, and progression mechanics operate as one mystery engine. The manuscript makes evidence-handling dramatic without abandoning terrain or bodies. | High |
+| Clarity / flow | 6/10 | Chapter 52, from “She asked for Brom’s blanket” through the year reduction; all `[[TIER-B: ...]]` blocks | The inferential chain is ultimately legible, but object labels and prior-history references accumulate faster than they are refreshed. The production notes are a major, objective break in narrative flow. | High |
+| Character attachment | 9/10 | Chapter 51, Teague and the injured bow in the hut; chapter 53, Teague’s “You kept my left” | Attachment comes through moral accounting: Teague refuses to turn from the field but owns the cost, Cael accepts thanks he cannot comfortably receive, and the crews gradually become one working body. | High |
+| Humor / warmth | 8/10 | Chapter 47, Brom’s seventh bowl; chapter 48, Brom defending “my mule”; chapter 53, the broad man discovering “Aye” | The humor arises from habits, affection, and material circumstances. Warmth accumulates through bowls, water, blankets, sticks, and hands rather than through tonal detours. | High |
+| Action / suspense | 9/10 | Chapter 50, the entire shelf action from the first declaration through Oryn knitting the fallen bow’s shin | The scene has legible terrain, opposed objectives, adaptation by both sides, injury, restraint, and aftermath. The watch remains human and tactically credible; the victory comes from width and differing risk across the line, not unexplained superiority. | High |
+| Progression payoff | 8/10 | Chapter 47, Teague teaching ground as a sum; chapter 50, the thirteenth; chapter 53, Cael aligning but refusing it | Competence progresses on several axes at once: field reading, team discipline, power knowledge, and ethical control. The new capability changes future options while its cost prevents a frictionless upgrade. | High |
+| Connection | 9/10 | Chapter 47’s missing stone and cord check through chapter 52’s blanket; repeated Vastin/Oryn/day counts in Cael’s logs | Nearly every practical detail becomes later evidence or consequence. The movement carries injuries, promises, measurements, and relationship shifts unusually well across chapters. | High |
+| Read-aloud quality | 8/10 | Chapter 48, the three-steps-and-a-stand descent; chapter 52, “Two pages... the whole of this blanket between them” | The prose has controlled rhythm and memorable physical metaphors. Repeated constructions such as “for the space of a breath” and “by the width of a finger” become noticeable over seven chapters, while unresolved brackets make the current file unsuitable for a clean performance. | High |
+
+## Manuscript-only consistency checks
+
+### Canon and reserved disclosures
+
+No editorial canon was supplied. I therefore found **no verified canon violation** and cannot determine whether any revelation exceeds a reserved boundary. The `TIER-B` notes explicitly identify protected text still awaiting restoration, but those notes are production artifacts, not canon evidence available to this cold read.
+
+### Physical state
+
+The visible injury and recovery chain is internally coherent across the supplied chapters. Vastin advances from twenty of thirty to twenty-six of thirty and refuses to use his wall; Teague’s recoil travels from wrist toward elbow and leaves the arm splinted; the bow’s previously good leg breaks, is knitted, boarded, and then bears weight only with a stick; Brom’s shoulder reaches its stated day; Cael’s collarbone bruise and old ribs affect later choices. I found no definite physical-state contradiction.
+
+One apparent count ambiguity deserves a check rather than a canon-error label. Chapter 47 says “when eleven people stood,” later “nobody among the twelve,” and chapter 49 says “There were twelve on it now.” Chapter 48’s “There were eleven now” appears capable of meaning eleven companions in addition to Cael, but the inclusion rule changes without being explicit. Confidence that this is a real numerical contradiction is low; confidence that it can momentarily confuse a cold reader is high.
+
+### Power limits
+
+The movement establishes and honors useful limits. Sigils disappear at a fixed stride inside the iron and return at the same stride; the shale-backs make use of a Path too dangerous; Teague’s Force requires contact, alignment, bracing, and bodily recoil; Cael’s acquired analogue listens at close range, needs a line through his frame, and threatens his old ribs. Cael repeatedly declines available power, so solutions remain grounded in observation, positioning, consent, and teamwork. No definite limit violation appears within this coverage.
+
+The distinction between a Path or sigil and Cael’s thirteen acquired things is inferable but not fully recoverable for a cold reader. His statement that “there was nothing in him that a line could take” can coexist with thirteen abilities because the log calls them “things that aren’t a Path,” but a brief local reminder would prevent this from reading as a contradiction.
+
+### Knowledge boundaries
+
+Knowledge is carefully partitioned. Cael tells Lira and later Brom about Teague’s thirteenth but withholds it from Teague; Teague recognizes Cael’s restraint without learning what Cael took; Vastin knows the two seals but refuses to name them; Karis records the presence of the seals without guessing; Karis also refuses to bridge her crossing data to the scraped statement without evidence. I found no clear leak across those boundaries.
+
+## Repair brief
+
+1. **Location:** Every `[[TIER-B: ...]]` block in chapters 49–53, especially chapter 50’s crossing sequence, chapter 51’s arm warning, chapter 52’s seal exchange, and chapter 53’s final lamp scene. **Observed issue:** Protected dialogue is absent and coordinator instructions remain inside the manuscript; the final scene withholds the movement’s actual closing exchange. **Effect on the reader:** Immersion breaks, speaker intent becomes uncertain at tactical and thematic turning points, and the movement reads as unfinished. **Proposed scope:** Restore only the approved protected lines, assign speakers consistently with the surrounding staging, remove the bracketed notes, and recheck the immediate paragraph joins; do not rewrite the scenes around them. **Strength to preserve:** The spare dialogue, moral restraint, and escalation created by what characters choose not to declare.
+
+2. **Location:** Chapter 47’s opening file inventory; chapter 48’s shale-back sequence; chapter 50 from “Brom walked” through the fall; chapter 51’s hut aftermath. **Observed issue:** Rapid switching among names and descriptors—“the boy,” “the tall man,” “the broad man,” “the bow,” “the old man,” and “the old woman”—makes identity tracking harder than the physical action itself, particularly because Cael is also young and Vastin is named only intermittently. **Effect on the reader:** A cold reader may attach an injury, choice, or relationship beat to the wrong person and lose some of the carefully carried consequences. **Proposed scope:** Add a small number of name-plus-role anchors at scene re-entry and before the fastest action, and make the eleven/twelve inclusion rule explicit once; avoid globally replacing the role language. **Strength to preserve:** The occupational, observational way Cael perceives people and the motif of individuals emerging from institutional roles.
+
+3. **Location:** Chapter 52, from “She asked for Brom’s blanket” through the two slips under the oldest row’s knot. **Observed issue:** The evidence scene introduces or reactivates many similar physical referents—hide, flat, clean sheet, copied sheets, taped rows, brass, folded paper, ruled leaf, columns, and seals—while also asking the reader to follow orientation, chronology, and inference. **Effect on the reader:** The crucial distinction between what is measured, what is copied, what is withheld, and what remains only a hypothesis can blur, reducing the force of Karis’s rigor. **Proposed scope:** Add two or three compact orienting reminders at the moments when the blanket’s function changes: first reconstruction, then dating, then institutional suppression. Keep the evidence and conclusions unchanged. **Strength to preserve:** The unusual pleasure of watching disciplined inquiry become dramatic action, especially Karis’s refusal to draw a line the evidence has not earned.

